All right.

204
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:43,000
Student, staff, and family share.

205
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:44,000
Who do we have with us today?

206
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,000
So we're going to introduce Principal Mensis.

207
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:52,000
As you know, as a board, transitions are kind of abundant in school systems.

208
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:56,000
And I would make an argument that maybe some transitions are more important than other ones.

209
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:02,000
So we have kids every year that transition from our elementary schools in grade five up to the middle school at grade six.

210
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:08,000
Coming together as four towns in a unique group that they're going to spend their first, you know, those three years together.

211
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:15,000
So we're trying to think of ways that we can connect these kids to the school before and also and maybe empower some of the students that are already there.

212
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:21,000
So Mr. Mensis is going to roll through a little bit of transitional programming that went on this summer here in Timberlaine.

213
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:22,000
All right.

214
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:23,000
Welcome, guys.

215
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:27,000
As you can tell, we have a number of participants this evening.

216
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:28,000
Oh, I'm sorry.

217
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
It's that number of participants.

218
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:31,000
There.

219
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:32,000
And one more for you.

220
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:33,000
Got it.

221
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:34,000
We'll make it all work.

222
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:35,000
Slide right over here.

223
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:36,000
All right.

224
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:37,000
She's like, no.

225
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:38,000
Yeah.

226
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:51,000
So just to give a quick overview, as you have in front of you, is we've talked about a lot of transitions stuff from middle school.

227
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:57,000
If you're going to six straight, it's a very big deal when you're coming from the four towns, going to take school, melting pot.

228
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:04,000
So this summer, we're talking to students and their experiences, as well as families, talking about how they're very nervous over the summer.

229
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:10,000
I would say the parents are a little bit nervous as well as they're looking at some former parents of kids coming to middle school.

230
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:11,000
It's a big deal.

231
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:21,000
We recognize that we had an untapped resource, which are our own students of student house of representatives, which actually dates back to working with Dr.

232
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:22,000
Preble.

233
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:23,000
I'm pretty sure the board recalls.

234
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:29,000
And the House of Representatives was born out of that work with Dr. Preble, our students, staff, and families.

235
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:35,000
We also have Student Senate, which has been an amazing group of young people in our programming for a long time.

236
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:40,000
And the two groups said maybe over the summer we could actually give tours to our students.

237
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
So the students led the tours themselves.

238
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
And they're going to share in a little bit.

239
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:49,000
But since they're just slightly less nervous than our now six-graders, I think they were first.

240
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:53,000
So they're expecting some softball type questions.

241
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:54,000
Please have those lined up.

242
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:58,000
I figured they could introduce themselves, name and town.

243
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:02,000
And then maybe some of their town reps could ask them a nice, easy question.

244
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:05,000
That's something working for you guys right now.

245
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:06,000
We'll start over there.

246
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
Just name and town you're from.

247
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,000
Hi, my name is Kingsley, and I'm from Sandown.

248
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:15,000
Hi, my name is Ryan Sturdy, and I'm from Pakistan.

249
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:18,000
My name is Ethan, and I'm from Plasha.

250
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:21,000
My name is Savannah, and I'm from Danville.

251
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
My name is Audrey, and I'm from Sandown.

252
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
So far, doing great, right?

253
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:29,000
So everyone here participated in the tour, right?

254
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:30,000
During the summer.

255
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:31,000
Awesome.

256
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:32,000
Well with parents.

257
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:33,000
Okay.

258
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:40,000
Any softball questions would be welcome from any town for your representative child.

259
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
So you all gave the tours, is that right?

260
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:43,000
Received.

261
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:44,000
You received the tours.

262
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:45,000
Okay.

263
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:51,000
Could you all just, whatever you want to do with this question, but what did you find most helpful with the tour?

264
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:56,000
Anybody wants to jump in about something that they felt was most helpful about the tour?

265
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:02,000
We need to know where things were in the middle school.

266
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:08,000
So do you feel like you had a better, like you knew more going into it, that that tour helped you find out where things were?

267
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:09,000
Yep.

268
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:14,000
Because we saw classrooms, gym, caf, bathrooms.

269
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:22,000
Did you guys have your schedules beforehand so you could find the classrooms that you were actually going to?

270
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:25,000
We got them at orientation.

271
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:26,000
Okay.

272
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:30,000
Which was after the tour.

273
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:31,000
Which was after the tour.

274
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:32,000
Okay.

275
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:35,000
Are you still getting lost in the two Sandown reps?

276
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:36,000
No.

277
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:39,000
I still have nightmares about getting lost.

278
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:40,000
Legitimately.

279
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:41,000
Legitimately.

280
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:42,000
Legitimately.

281
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:46,000
To be fair though, do you get lost in middle school in the first day or two a little bit?

282
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:47,000
Yeah.

283
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:50,000
Because of the tours.

284
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
But it's a little bit quicker.

285
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:53,000
Nice.

286
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
Melissa, did you have something?

287
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:00,000
A common middle school anxiety point, getting into your lockers.

288
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:06,000
Did they go over how to work a lock on the lockers during the tours?

289
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:07,000
No.

290
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:08,000
Well, we'll do.

291
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:11,000
So, actually that sounds like that's on the list.

292
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:12,000
Yeah.

293
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:13,000
Nice.

294
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
The hard part of that was that we'd have to have the locker combinations.

295
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:18,000
Well, but maybe some practice, maybe some practice locks.

296
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:19,000
We can have some practice locks.

297
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:20,000
For sure.

298
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:21,000
Hey, this is great feedback.

299
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:22,000
Jack, did you have something?

300
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:23,000
Yeah, I do.

301
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:24,000
To the end of the neck.

302
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,000
Who's your favorite school board rep?

303
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:29,000
How do you go on?

304
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:30,000
Don.

305
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:40,000
Do you guys all feel comfortable with being in school with bigger and older kids?

